Subject: [PATCH] iommu/mediatek: mt6797: stop labelling every fault as larb0

mt6797_data carried `.larbid_remap = {{0}}` with the comment "linear mapping;
larb0 only". That was true when only the display was behind the M4U. It became
a lie when the camera arrived on larb2, and the array is zero-initialised, so
the fault handler's

	fault_larb = data->plat_data->larbid_remap[fault_larb][sub_comm];

turned every larb the hardware reported into 0. A CAM_SV0 translation fault
printed as

	fault type=0x5 iova=0x2 pa=0x0 master=0x118(larb=0 port=6) layer=0 write

and larb0 port6 is M4U_PORT_MDP_WROT0 -- a display block that was not running.
The real decode of master=0x118 is larb2 port6 = M4U_PORT_CAM_SV0:

	F_MMU_INT_ID_LARB_ID(0x118) = (0x118 >> 7) & 0x7 = 2
	F_MMU_INT_ID_PORT_ID(0x118) = (0x118 >> 2) & 0x1f = 6

The raw master ID in the message was right all along, so the information was
recoverable by hand -- but only by someone who already suspected the table.
It cost a wrong-path investigation on #74.

MT6797 has seven larbs (the vendor's gM4uPort[] spans larb0..larb6) and this
tree now instantiates larb0, larb2 and larb5. Eight entries are listed anyway,
because F_MMU_INT_ID_LARB_ID() masks to three bits: an impossible id 7 should
print as 7 rather than silently as 0, which is the whole point of the fix.

Same form mt8173 and mt6795 already use.

--- a/drivers/iommu/mtk_iommu.c
+++ b/drivers/iommu/mtk_iommu.c
@@ -1570,9 +1570,22 @@
  * gate. Claiming HAS_BCLK here would fail the probe looking for a clock the
  * SoC does not have.
  *
- * Only larb0 is listed. The hardware has seven (vendor gM4uPort[] spans
- * larb0..larb6), but larb0 is the only one this tree instantiates, and the
- * display pipeline is entirely behind it.
+ * larbid_remap is the identity over all eight indices the fault handler can
+ * produce. It used to be {{0}} with the comment "linear mapping; larb0 only",
+ * which was true when only the display was wired up and became a lie when the
+ * camera arrived on larb2. The array is zero-initialised, so every larb the
+ * hardware reported came back as larb0: a CAM_SV0 fault printed as
+ *
+ *   fault type=0x5 iova=0x2 pa=0x0 master=0x118(larb=0 port=6) ... write
+ *
+ * and larb0 port6 is MDP_WROT0, a display block that was not running. The raw
+ * master ID was right all along; only the parenthesised decode was wrong, so
+ * it cost a wrong-path investigation rather than a wrong fix. See #74/#75.
+ *
+ * MT6797 has seven larbs (vendor gM4uPort[] spans larb0..larb6) and this tree
+ * now instantiates larb0, larb2 and larb5. Eight entries are listed anyway
+ * because F_MMU_INT_ID_LARB_ID() masks to 3 bits: an impossible id 7 should
+ * print as 7, not silently as 0.
  */
 static const struct mtk_iommu_plat_data mt6797_data = {
 	.m4u_plat     = M4U_MT6797,
@@ -1598,7 +1611,7 @@
 	.banks_enable = {true},
 	.iova_region  = single_domain,
 	.iova_region_nr = ARRAY_SIZE(single_domain),
-	.larbid_remap = {{0}}, /* Linear mapping; larb0 only. */
+	.larbid_remap = {{0}, {1}, {2}, {3}, {4}, {5}, {6}, {7}}, /* Linear. */
 };
 
 static const struct mtk_iommu_plat_data mt8167_data = {
